From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent: never pin the backhaul BSSID in config

wifi_set_iface_bssid() persisted the BSSID the bSTA happened to associate to,
so the next boot bound it to that exact address. BSSIDs are not stable: each
BSS address comes from a counter incremented in interface preparation order
(/usr/share/ucode/wifi/iface.uc), so it moves whenever the interface set
changes - and, measured on 2026-07-22, even across two reboots with an
identical config, where the controller's 6 GHz backhaul went
bc:2f -> bd:2f -> bc:2f.

A pinned bSTA then finds nothing and only scans, taking the node out of the
mesh. That happened four times in one afternoon.

The in-memory copy is kept, so the agent still knows what it is associated to.
Only the config is left alone, and an empty value is written so a node
carrying a stale pin heals itself on the first association.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/config.c
+++ b/src/config.c
@@ -315,10 +315,28 @@
 	if (!section)
 		goto out_pkg;
 
-	if (bssid && !hwaddr_is_zero(bssid))
-		hwaddr_ntoa(bssid, bssid_str);
+	/* PATCH 984: never pin the backhaul BSSID in config.
+	 *
+	 * Persisting the BSSID we happen to be associated to makes the next boot
+	 * bind the bSTA to that exact address. BSSIDs are not stable: without
+	 * `option macaddr` each BSS address comes from a counter incremented in
+	 * interface preparation order (/usr/share/ucode/wifi/iface.uc), so it
+	 * moves whenever the interface set changes - and, measured on 2026-07-22,
+	 * even across two reboots with an identical config (the controller's
+	 * 6 GHz backhaul went bc:2f -> bd:2f -> bc:2f). A pinned bSTA then finds
+	 * nothing and only scans, which takes the whole node out of the mesh. It
+	 * happened four times in one afternoon.
+	 *
+	 * The in-memory copy below is kept - the agent still knows what it is
+	 * associated to. Only the config is left alone, so the bSTA follows the
+	 * SSID and reassociates wherever that SSID currently lives.
+	 *
+	 * An empty value is written rather than nothing at all, so a node that
+	 * already carries a stale pin heals itself on the first association.
+	 */
+	bssid_str[0] = '\0';
 
-	dbg("%s: setting bssid to %s\n", __func__, bssid_str);
+	dbg("%s: clearing persisted bssid (follow SSID instead)\n", __func__);
 
 	ret = set_value(ctx, pkg, section, "bssid", bssid_str, UCI_TYPE_STRING);
 	uci_commit(ctx, &pkg, false);
